-- allium: 3
-- epics.allium
--
-- Scope: Epic lifecycle and MCP epic tools
-- Excludes: Task lifecycle (see tasks.allium), domain model (see core.allium)

use "./core.allium" as core
use "./store-split.allium" as split
use "./tasks.allium" as tasks
use "./settings.allium" as settings
use "./pr-workflow.allium" as pr
use "./workspaces.allium" as workspaces

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Config
------------------------------------------------------------

-- Managed-feed configuration surface. These are app-level settings (not fields
-- on any epic): two feed scripts, each with an optional poll interval. They
-- drive ProvisionManagedEpics, which materialises the corresponding managed
-- epic tree. All four default to "unset": a null command means that subtree is
-- not provisioned at all (provisioning is opt-in). When a command is set but
-- its interval is null, the epic falls back to core/config.default_feed_interval,
-- exactly as for hand-made feed epics.
--
-- NOTE — these four settings are editable ONLY over MCP, via
-- managed_feed_config (a call with no arguments reads them back). There is no
-- in-app TUI editor. On save, that surface persists the settings and re-fires
-- ProvisionManagedEpics, so a newly-enabled feed provisions its epic tree
-- without a restart.
config {
    reviews_feed_command: String? = null         -- script emitting the involved-PR feed (see feeds.allium); null = reviews subtree not provisioned
    reviews_feed_interval_secs: Integer? = null   -- poll cadence for the reviews_parent root; null falls back to default_feed_interval
    cve_feed_command: String? = null              -- script emitting the security/CVE feed; null = cve epic not provisioned
    cve_feed_interval_secs: Integer? = null       -- poll cadence for the cve root; null falls back to default_feed_interval
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rules
------------------------------------------------------------

-- == Editor ==

rule EditEpic {
    when: EditEpic(epic)

    -- Opens the user's editor — resolved by the shared rule stated with
    -- core/config.editor_fallback, the same resolution EditTask uses — with a
    -- structured file containing the epic's
    -- title, description, feed_command and feed_interval_secs. The file
    -- uses "--- SECTION ---" delimiters (TITLE, DESCRIPTION,
    -- FEED_COMMAND, FEED_INTERVAL_SECS).
    -- The user edits the file; on save, fields are parsed back. Header-shaped
    -- lines in a value are escaped and unescaped exactly as EditTask states.
    -- For title and description, an empty field retains the previous
    -- value. For feed_command and feed_interval_secs, an empty field
    -- clears the value (sets it to null) — this is how feeds are turned
    -- off from the editor.
    -- FEED_INTERVAL_SECS accepts the shared interval-literal grammar
    -- (core.allium: "How a human types an interval"), so "10m" and "600" are
    -- the same edit. It is written back as bare seconds, so a suffixed value
    -- reappears unsuffixed on the next open. A bare integer means seconds.
    -- FEED_INTERVAL_SECS otherwise follows the same parse rule as EditTask's
    -- enum-ish sections: an empty section and an unparseable section are
    -- treated identically — the section yields no value, so the field
    -- is cleared. Unparseable input additionally records a parse error, and
    -- all accumulated errors are surfaced together as a status message
    -- ("Edit accepted with parse errors — ..."); the edit is still applied.
    -- Status, plan_path, sort_order and auto_dispatch are not editable
    -- via the epic editor.
    --
    -- The interval grammar and the interval FLOOR are enforced in different
    -- places, and this section is where a user meets the seam. The grammar
    -- only parses, so "10s" is a SUCCESSFUL parse — no parse error is
    -- recorded — and is then refused on save by the floor
    -- (core.allium: "Interval literals", CLAIM 2). A refusal is not the
    -- parse-error path above: the save goes through UpdateEpic as one
    -- validated call, so a refused interval rejects the WHOLE edit. No field
    -- is applied, not even an unrelated title change in the same session, and
    -- the validation error is surfaced as an error status message rather than
    -- the "Edit accepted with parse errors" one.
    --
    -- A refused edit MUST leave the board showing the stored epic. Reporting
    -- an error and then rendering the refused value is the worst of both: the
    -- user is told the save failed while looking at evidence it succeeded, and
    -- the display only self-corrects on the next DB refresh.

    let original_feed_command = epic.feed_command

    ensures:
        let edited = EditorSession(epic)
        -- A sub-floor interval refuses the whole edit, so every clause below
        -- is guarded on acceptance: when the interval is refused NO field
        -- changes, and the epic the board shows is the stored one.
        let accepted = edited.feed_interval_secs = null
                       or seconds(edited.feed_interval_secs) >= core/config.min_feed_interval
        if accepted and edited.title != "": epic.title = edited.title
        if accepted and edited.description != "": epic.description = edited.description
        -- feed fields clear on empty rather than retaining
        if accepted:
            if edited.feed_command != "": epic.feed_command = edited.feed_command
            else: epic.feed_command = null
        if accepted:
            if edited.feed_interval_secs != null:
                epic.feed_interval_secs = edited.feed_interval_secs
            else: epic.feed_interval_secs = null
        -- Feed-ownership take-over offer: only when this edit actually
        -- CHANGES feed_command to a non-empty value (not a clear, not an
        -- edit that left it untouched), and only when a core/PollOwner row
        -- already names a host other than this one. See @guidance for the
        -- prompt this sits behind and feeds.allium: OverrideFeedOwner for
        -- what accepting it does.
        if accepted and edited.feed_command != "" and edited.feed_command != original_feed_command:
            let existing_owner = core/PollOwner{scope: epic, scope_id: epic.id}
            if exists existing_owner and existing_owner.host != core/local_host():
                FeedOwnerTakeoverOffered(epic: epic, current_owner: existing_owner.host)

    @guidance
        -- Unlike EditTask, the epic editor does not expose status.
        -- Epic status is derived from subtask states via the
        -- EpicStatusRecalculation invariant and is not directly
        -- editable by the user.
        -- updated_at is touched automatically by the database layer on
        -- any patch_epic call, so the rule does not assert it directly.
        -- The editor opens in a separate tmux window; tmux focus
        -- switches to that window while the TUI keeps running in
        -- its original window. On editor exit the window closes and
        -- focus returns to the TUI. At most one editor session is
        -- live at a time — a second edit while an editor is already
        -- open is a no-op with a status message.
        --
        -- == Feed-ownership take-over prompt ==
        --
        -- FeedOwnerTakeoverOffered is presented as a y/n status-bar
        -- confirmation, the same shape tasks.allium's ConfirmDone /
        -- dispatch.allium's ConfirmTrustRepo already use: "This feed is
        -- currently owned by <current_owner's host label> — take over
        -- polling? [y/N]". Accepting emits feeds.allium:
        -- OverrideFeedOwner(epic); declining emits nothing further. Either
        -- way the feed_command edit above has ALREADY been applied and
        -- saved by this point — the prompt only decides ownership, never
        -- whether the edit itself takes effect.
        --
        -- No prompt at all in the common cases: a brand-new feed epic (no
        -- PollOwner row yet — the next FeedTick claims it normally), an
        -- edit that leaves feed_command unchanged or clears it, or an epic
        -- this host already owns.
}

-- == Epic Lifecycle ==

rule CreateEpic {
    when: CreateEpic(title, description?, sort_order?, parent?,
                     feed_command?, feed_interval_secs?)

    -- The universal feed-cadence floor (core.allium: "Interval literals",
    -- CLAIM 2). A value below it is rejected with a validation error naming
    -- the field and the minimum. Creation is bound as tightly as update:
    -- validating only the update path would leave an epic able to be BORN
    -- busy-looping.
    requires:
        if feed_interval_secs != null:
            seconds(feed_interval_secs) >= core/config.min_feed_interval

    -- parent, when given, must refer to an existing epic. Checked before the
    -- insert, so a bad parent yields a not-found error and no epic at all.
    requires:
        if parent != null: exists parent

    ensures: core/Epic.created(
        title: title,
        description: description,
        status: backlog,
        sort_order: sort_order,
        parent: parent,
        feed_command: feed_command,
        feed_interval_secs: feed_interval_secs,
        created_at: now,
        updated_at: now
    )

    -- A new backlog child changes the parent's sub_epics set, so the
    -- parent is recalculated immediately after the insert (e.g. a Done parent
    -- with a freshly-attached Backlog child regresses right away).
    ensures:
        if parent != null: RecalculateEpicStatus(parent)

    @guidance
        -- Invariant: the returned Epic reflects every field the caller
        -- supplied. A caller must never see an Epic reporting a field as
        -- unset when it passed a value for it.
        --
        -- CreateEpicViaMcp (below) is not a second implementation: both rules
        -- witness the one service entry point, which is where the
        -- parent-existence check lives. The parent recalculation is the
        -- module's own, inside create_epic: the service asks for no second
        -- one (store-split.allium, X3). The two
        -- rules differ only in which parameters the surface exposes.
        --
        -- The create is one write: sort_order, feed_command and
        -- feed_interval_secs ride in the created row, so a failure leaves no
        -- half-configured epic and a retry cannot duplicate one.
        --
        -- When parent is provided, this creates a sub-epic nested inside
        -- the given parent. Root epics (parent = null) appear on the main board;
        -- sub-epics appear only inside their parent's epic view.
        --
        -- This grouping is bypassed when the board is in flattened mode
        -- (see board-layout.allium: "Flattened Mode"), but only in the Running,
        -- Review and Done columns: there epic cards are hidden and every
        -- descendant task surfaces directly in its status column. Backlog is
        -- excluded from flattening — it is the column read at the epic level,
        -- so epic cards remain visible there and epic-owned tasks stay inside
        -- them.
        --
        -- Outside flattened mode an epic card is not confined to one column:
        -- it is drawn in every column where the epic's subtree has visible
        -- work (see board-layout.allium: "Epic Card Placement").
}

-- There is no batch wrap-up for an epic. Each subtask's agent wraps its
-- own work up through the MCP wrap_up tool (see pr-workflow.allium);
-- there is no board-driven merge queue that sequences rebases across an
-- epic's review tasks.

rule MoveEpicForward {
    when: MoveEpicStatus(epic, forward)

    requires: epic.status != done

    let next = next_status(epic.status)

    ensures: epic.status = next
    ensures: epic.updated_at = now
    ensures:
        if next = done:
            epic.completed_at = now
            for task in epic.subtasks where current_agent(task).tmux_window != null:
                current_agent(task).tmux_window = null

    @guidance
        -- Uses the same next() progression as tasks:
        --   backlog -> running -> review -> done.
        -- Done is terminal for forward movement.
        -- When moving to done, all subtask tmux windows are killed
        -- to clean up active agent sessions.
        -- Cleanup is direct-subtasks only, unlike DeleteEpic which
        -- recurses into sub-epic descendants.
        -- Unlike MoveTaskForward (which excludes done from forward
        -- movement entirely), MoveEpicForward allows manual forward
        -- movement directly into done. epic.completed_at = now is the same
        -- completion stamp tasks.allium's ConfirmDone applies, and the Done
        -- column reads it descending: an epic that has finished sorts by its
        -- own completion time in preference to the derived subtask key
        -- (board-layout.allium, "Done Column Ordering").
}

rule MoveEpicBackward {
    when: MoveEpicStatus(epic, backward)

    requires: epic.status != backlog

    let prev = prev_status(epic.status)

    ensures: epic.status = prev
    ensures: epic.updated_at = now

    @guidance
        -- Uses the same prev() progression as tasks:
        --   done -> review -> running -> backlog.
        -- Backlog is terminal for backward movement.
        -- Leaving done clears nothing, mirroring tasks.allium's
        -- MoveTaskBackward: epic.sort_order is manual ordering that no status
        -- write touches, and epic.completed_at records the last completion,
        -- which moving backward does not unmake.
}

rule ConfirmDeleteEpic {
    when: ConfirmDeleteEpic(epic)

    -- Every task anywhere in the epic's subtree, at any depth, is done. An
    -- epic with no tasks at all qualifies.
    requires: epic.subtree_tasks.all(t => t.status = done)

    -- Prompts the user with "Delete epic <title> and subtasks? [y/n]".
    -- Pressing y emits DeleteEpic(epic); any other key cancels.
    ensures: ConfirmationShown(epic, "Delete epic and subtasks?")

    @guidance
        -- Reached from 'x' on a single selected epic (tasks.allium:
        -- DeleteKeyRouting). A multi-selection containing epics goes through
        -- BatchDelete instead, which applies the same guard per epic and
        -- refuses the whole batch if any epic (or any selected task) fails.
        --
        -- Why the whole subtree and not only direct subtasks: delete is
        -- permanent and cascades to every descendant, so a guard that looked
        -- one level down would let a grouped or nested epic take unfinished
        -- work with it. The guard is the same "finished" test a single task
        -- delete applies (DeleteTask requires done), lifted to the subtree.
        -- Sub-epics themselves are not tested by status; an empty sub-epic,
        -- or one whose tasks are all done, does not block the parent.
}

rule DeleteEpic {
    when: DeleteEpic(epic)

    -- decodable(t): the deleting client could decode t's row. A row it could
    -- not decode is dropped from its reads (observability.allium:
    -- BulkReadSkipsUndecodableRow) but is still in the subtree, and its status
    -- is unknown, so it never counts as done.
    requires: epic.subtree_tasks.all(t => decodable(t) and t.status = done)

    -- subtree_depth(epic): how many levels of sub-epics hang below epic (0
    -- for an epic with none), counting each epic once, so a loop that got in
    -- some other way still has a finite answer. A subtree deeper than the
    -- store's recursive delete reaches is refused rather than deleted down to
    -- that level, which would strand the rows below it.
    requires: subtree_depth(epic) <= core/config.max_epic_depth

    -- The epics this delete removes: the epic itself and every descendant.
    -- A feed epic among them is being reset, not pruned.
    let doomed_epics = epic.subtree_epics + epic

    -- Deletes epic and all linked subtasks and sub-epics (recursively)
    ensures:
        for task in epic.subtree_tasks:
            if task.worktree != null:
                not exists task.worktree
    -- A deleted feed task is retired under its feed epic when that feed epic
    -- survives the delete; when the feed epic is itself in the deleted
    -- subtree there is nothing to retire under, and its existing records go
    -- with it (below).
    ensures:
        for task in epic.subtree_tasks:
            if task.external_id != null
               and task.epic.nearest_feed_epic != null
               and task.epic.nearest_feed_epic not in doomed_epics
               and not exists core/RetiredFeedItem{feed_epic: task.epic.nearest_feed_epic, external_id: task.external_id}:
                core/RetiredFeedItem.created(
                    feed_epic:   task.epic.nearest_feed_epic,
                    external_id: task.external_id,
                    retired_at:  now
                )
    ensures:
        for task in epic.subtree_tasks:
            not exists task
            tasks/TaskRowDeleted(task: task)
    ensures:
        for item in core/RetiredFeedItems where item.feed_epic in doomed_epics:
            not exists item
    ensures:
        for sub_epic in epic.subtree_epics:
            not exists sub_epic
    ensures: not exists epic

    @guidance
        -- Permanent removal, like DeleteTask: there is no archived state for an
        -- epic to fall back to. The guard lives on the rule as well as on the
        -- confirmation because DeleteEpic has internal callers besides the
        -- TUI — FlattenEpic and the feed's empty repo-group cleanup
        -- (feeds.allium: FlatFeedReconcile, delete_if_empty_repo_group) — and
        -- every one of them deletes only epics with no tasks left, which the
        -- guard admits.
        --
        -- ENFORCED TWICE, DELIBERATELY. EpicService::delete_epic
        -- (src/service/epics.rs) checks this guard client-side, over
        -- the Store's subscription view, before ever calling the store —
        -- that is what lets a refusal be instant and say why. But the view
        -- can be incomplete, and a delete is permanent: an
        -- epic visible only through own_creations can pass that client
        -- check while holding
        -- non-done tasks the view never delivered. So the same guard is
        -- checked again inside the delete_epic reducer itself
        -- (spacetime/module/src/tasks_epics.rs::validate_epic_deletable), over the store's true row set, and
        -- the reducer refuses (returns an error, deletes nothing) rather
        -- than trusting the caller. The client check is the responsive
        -- path; the reducer check is the one that actually holds against
        -- every board, not just the caller's own subscription.
        --
        -- THE DEPTH GUARD IS THE REDUCER'S ALONE. delete_epic and
        -- batch_delete refuse a subtree nested more than
        -- core/config.max_epic_depth levels deep, with "epic <id>: cannot
        -- delete a subtree nested more than 64 levels deep", before deleting
        -- anything. The client pre-check does not measure depth, so such
        -- a refusal comes after the subtree's teardown has run, as for the
        -- reopen race below; a tree that deep is not one the board builds.
        --
        -- PRE-CHECK BEFORE ANY SIDE EFFECT, as for DeleteTask
        -- (tasks.allium). The subtree's teardown — every worktree, tmux
        -- window and branch — runs client-side
        -- before the reducer is called, so the reducer guard alone would
        -- refuse too late. Before ANY of them starts, the delete flow
        -- re-reads the TRUE status of every task in the subtree from the
        -- store, not the board's subscription view, and proceeds only if all
        -- are done. A failed pre-check refuses the delete with an error
        -- (DeleteEpicRefused below) and touches NOTHING anywhere in the
        -- subtree: no teardown, no row removal, no
        -- retirement; the board is refreshed from the store, restoring the
        -- cards it dropped optimistically. A passing pre-check runs the flow
        -- unchanged: best-effort subtree teardown, then the guarded reducer.
        -- The reducer stays the final authority, and the residual is the
        -- one DeleteTask's guidance states: a subtree task reopened between
        -- the pre-check read and the reducer call is refused by the reducer
        -- after the teardown has run — the only case in which
        -- "deletes nothing" covers just the rows and their RetiredFeedItems.
        --
        -- Deletion is performed in a single database transaction that
        -- walks parent pointers depth-first, deleting each
        -- epic's tasks then the epic row itself. Worktrees and tmux
        -- windows are cleaned up best-effort for every subtask in that
        -- same subtree — not just the directly-owned ones — before the DB
        -- call. The two must agree: the DB delete drops the whole subtree,
        -- so a cleanup that covered only direct children would delete a
        -- nested subtask's row while leaving its worktree, branch and tmux
        -- window on disk with nothing left referencing them.
        -- This rule stays exempt from WorktreeReleaseIsGated's (c)
        -- (tasks.allium): it removes the rows in one operation, leaving
        -- nothing that could hold a pointer.
        --
        -- Retirement is written in the same transaction as the row deletes,
        -- for the reason DeleteTask gives: a cycle landing between the two
        -- would re-insert. The feed epic is read from each task's chain as it
        -- stands before the delete (core/Epic.nearest_feed_epic).
        --
        -- Deleting a feed epic is a reset. Its RetiredFeedItems are dropped
        -- along with it, in the same delete, so if the same feed is set up again
        -- — or a managed epic is re-provisioned (ProvisionManagedEpics) — every
        -- still-open upstream item the user had deleted reappears. Accepted:
        -- keeping records for an epic that no longer exists would suppress
        -- items under a feed nobody can see or edit.
        --
        -- If the currently active view is on the deleted epic, the TUI
        -- exits back to the parent view.
}

rule DeleteEpicRefused {
    when: DeleteEpic(epic)

    -- Evaluated over the store's TRUE rows (DeleteEpic's pre-check), before
    -- any subtree teardown begins.
    requires: not epic.subtree_tasks.all(t => decodable(t) and t.status = done)

    -- Not the arm for a subtree that is all done but nested too deep
    -- (DeleteEpic's subtree_depth guard): the pre-check does not measure
    -- depth, so that refusal comes from the store's reducer alone, after the
    -- teardown, with the reducer's own wording.

    -- The refusal names what blocks the delete. The blocking task is the
    -- LOWEST-ID readable subtree task that is not done — not the first one a
    -- subtree walk happens to reach — so the same epic always names the same
    -- task. The tasks the client could not decode are counted and named by id.
    let unreadable = filter(epic.subtree_tasks, t => not decodable(t))
    let undone = filter(epic.subtree_tasks, t => decodable(t) and t.status != done)
    let blocking_task = min_by(undone, t => t.id)    -- null when undone is empty

    ensures:
        for task in epic.subtree_tasks:
            task.worktree = task.worktree
            current_agent(task).tmux_window = current_agent(task).tmux_window
        UserInformed(
            about: delete_refused,
            epic: epic,
            blocking_task: blocking_task,
            unreadable_count: unreadable.count
        )

    @guidance
        -- The refusal arm of DeleteEpic's pre-check: the epic, every
        -- sub-epic and subtree task, their worktrees, tmux windows and
        -- branches are all left as they are, and the board is
        -- refreshed from the store. See DeleteTask's guidance (tasks.allium)
        -- for the narrow pre-check-to-reducer race that remains.
        --
        -- WHAT THE MESSAGE SAYS. A refusal that only says "a task is not done"
        -- leaves the user hunting through a deep subtree for it, so the
        -- message names the blocker:
        --   - a readable blocker: its id, title and status, e.g.
        --     cannot delete epic: task #123 "Title" (status: running) in its subtree is not done
        --   - only unreadable tasks block: how many, and their ids, e.g.
        --     cannot delete epic: 2 task(s) in its subtree could not be read (ids #7, #9)
        --   - both: the readable blocker as above, plus the unreadable count
        --     and ids. The readable blocker leads because it is the one the
        --     user can act on.
        -- Lowest id is what the store's own delete_epic guard names too
        -- (spacetime/module/src/tasks_epics.rs::first_undone_task_in), so a
        -- refusal from either check points at the same task.
        --
        -- WHY UNREADABLE TASKS BLOCK. A row the client cannot decode is
        -- skipped by its reads, so it is invisible on the board but still in
        -- the subtree. Its status is unknown, and delete is permanent; treating
        -- an unknown status as done would let the delete take work nobody
        -- could see. Refusing, and saying the rows could not be read, is the
        -- only honest answer.
        --
        -- Only the client pre-check (EpicService::ensure_deletable) composes
        -- this message. The store's delete_epic reducer is unchanged and
        -- still has the final say over its own true rows.
}

-- == MCP Epic Tools ==

rule CreateEpicViaMcp {
    when: McpCreateEpic(title, description?, sort_order?, parent_epic_id?,
                        workspace_id?, visibility?)

    -- parent_epic_id, when given, must refer to an existing epic.
    requires:
        if parent_epic_id != null: exists core/Epic{id: parent_epic_id}

    ensures: core/Epic.created(
        title: title,
        description: description,
        status: backlog,
        sort_order: sort_order,
        parent: if parent_epic_id = null: null else: core/Epic{id: parent_epic_id},
        workspace: if parent_epic_id != null: core/Epic{id: parent_epic_id}.workspace
                   else: core/Workspace{id: workspace_id},
        visibility: visibility ?? public,
        created_at: now,
        updated_at: now
    )

    @guidance
        -- workspace_id is required for a root epic and refused with
        -- parent_epic_id (the sub-epic takes its parent's). The handler
        -- refuses both before it calls the store; the store then checks the
        -- caller may create there (workspaces.allium: CreateEpicInWorkspace).
        -- feed_command and feed_interval_secs are NOT exposed by the
        -- MCP create_epic tool — even though the underlying service
        -- accepts them, the handler hardcodes them to null. Feeds are
        -- configured after creation either from the TUI (via the epic
        -- editor, EditEpic) or via UpdateEpicViaMcp.
}

-- One epic is read through list_epics with epic_id: there is no separate
-- get_epic tool (docs/mcp-tool-design.md, rule 1: a get and a list of the same
-- entity are one capability). The two forms are separate rules because their
-- replies differ: the list gives one row per epic, the epic_id form the full
-- record. list_work_packages(work_package_id) is the same shape.
rule GetEpicViaMcp {
    when: McpListEpics(epic_id?, parent_epic_id?, recursive?, query?, limit?)

    requires: epic_id != null
    requires: exists core/Epic{id: epic_id}
    -- epic_id picks one epic, so no filter can narrow it further. A call
    -- that sets epic_id together with any other argument is invalid params
    -- (-32602), not a silent ignore.
    requires: parent_epic_id = null and recursive = null and query = null
              and limit = null

    -- Returns the Epic record for epic_id along with a subtask summary
    -- (done_count, total_count) rolled up exactly as ListEpicsViaMcp
    -- rolls it up.
    ensures: EpicRetrieved(epic_id)

    @guidance
        -- Returns the epic's core fields (title, description,
        -- status, plan_path, sort_order, parent_epic_id, feed_command,
        -- feed_interval_secs, created_at, updated_at), its workspace and
        -- visibility, plus a "Subtasks: D/T done" line where D counts subtasks with
        -- status = done and T is the total.
        -- The counts come from EpicService::get_epic_with_progress, which
        -- reuses the same rollup as list_epics_with_progress
        -- (ListEpicsViaMcp) so the one-epic reply, the list rows and the
        -- board never disagree: for a group_by_repo epic the tasks of all descendant
        -- sub-epics are aggregated recursively; for any other epic only
        -- the direct subtasks are counted. Without the shared rollup a
        -- group_by_repo epic whose tasks all live in per-repo sub-epics
        -- reports 0/0 here while list_epics shows the real totals.
        -- An unknown epic_id returns ServiceError::NotFound, surfaced as
        -- the -32001 not-found code (distinct from -32602, which means
        -- invalid params).
        --
        -- parent_epic_id follows the same "absent line means unset"
        -- convention as plan_path, sort_order, feed_command and
        -- feed_interval_secs: when set, the response includes a
        -- "Parent: <id> <title>" line naming the parent epic; when null,
        -- the line is omitted entirely rather than shown empty. If
        -- parent_epic_id is set but the parent epic cannot be resolved —
        -- not expected to happen, since DeleteEpic (above) only ever
        -- removes a parent together with its whole subtree — the line
        -- still renders as "Parent: <id>" without a title, rather than
        -- being omitted: a set-but-unresolvable reference must never
        -- read the same as no parent at all.
}

rule ListEpicsViaMcp {
    when: McpListEpics(epic_id?, parent_epic_id?, recursive?, query?, limit?)

    -- With epic_id set the call is GetEpicViaMcp (above).
    requires: epic_id = null
    requires:
        if parent_epic_id != null: exists core/Epic{id: parent_epic_id}
    -- recursive narrows nothing on its own: the unfiltered list is already
    -- every epic.
    requires:
        if recursive = true: parent_epic_id != null

    -- Returns Epic records with per-epic subtask progress
    -- (done_count, total_count): every epic when parent_epic_id is null; the
    -- epics whose parent is parent_epic_id when recursive is false; every
    -- descendant of parent_epic_id (not parent_epic_id itself) when it is true.
    -- A limit of zero is rejected as invalid params (-32602).
    requires:
        if limit != null: limit >= 1

    -- When query is set, only epics matching it are kept, after the scope
    -- above is applied (the two are ANDed).
    ensures: EpicsListed(parent_epic_id: parent_epic_id, recursive: recursive,
                         query: query, limit: limit)

    @guidance
        -- query is split on whitespace into words; an epic matches when
        -- every word is a case-insensitive substring of its title or its
        -- description. A blank query is the same as none. This is the same
        -- rule as list_tasks (ListTasksViaMcp in mcp-task-tools.allium). The
        -- progress counts of a matching epic are unchanged. Nothing matching
        -- gives the usual "No epics found".
        -- limit (optional, no default) keeps the first `limit` rows after the
        -- scope and query are applied; when rows were cut the reply ends with
        -- "Showing <limit> of <total>. Narrow your query or filters." The
        -- same rule as list_tasks.
        --
        -- All arguments are optional. With neither it returns every epic in
        -- the database along with done/total subtask counts for each. An
        -- unknown parent_epic_id is not found (-32001); recursive without
        -- parent_epic_id is invalid params (-32602).
        -- The response is one line per epic carrying the
        -- epic id, the title, the progress fraction, then
        -- "[workspace <id>, <visibility>]", then a plan indicator
        -- (when a plan is attached) followed by a status indicator (when
        -- the status is not backlog), then " (parent:<id> <parent title>)" when the epic
        -- has a parent, and finally the description. The parent is on every row so
        -- a caller can rebuild the tree from one call instead of one
        -- list_epics(epic_id) per epic. The id
        -- is included because it is how the caller addresses the epic in
        -- follow-up calls (list_epics with epic_id, update_epic, create_task).
        -- An empty result returns an informational "No epics found"
        -- message rather than an error.
}

-- The update_epic reply is "<epic ref> updated (fields)", e.g.
-- `Epic 314 ("Dispatch") updated (title)` (mcp-task-tools.allium:
-- RepliesNameWhatTheyTouch). When the call changes the title, the reply shows
-- the new one.
rule UpdateEpicViaMcp {
    when: McpUpdateEpic(epic, title?, description?, status?, plan_path?, sort_order?,
                        feed_command?, feed_interval_secs?, group_by_repo?,
                        feed_append_only?, parent_epic_id?, visibility?,
                        workspace_id?)

    requires: title != null or description != null or status != null
              or plan_path != null or sort_order != null
              or feed_command != omitted
              or feed_interval_secs != omitted or group_by_repo != null
              or feed_append_only != null
              or parent_epic_id != omitted
              or visibility != null or workspace_id != null

    -- The universal feed-cadence floor (core.allium: "Interval literals",
    -- CLAIM 2). A value below it is rejected with a validation error naming
    -- the field and the minimum. Explicit null still clears the field: that
    -- means "inherit config.default_feed_interval", which is itself at or
    -- above the floor, so clearing can never sink below it.
    requires:
        if feed_interval_secs != omitted and feed_interval_secs != null:
            seconds(feed_interval_secs) >= core/config.min_feed_interval

    -- parent_epic_id validation: parent must exist and no cycle may be introduced
    --
    -- THE STORE ENFORCES THE DATA CHECKS IN THIS RULE (store-split.allium
    -- S1): the patch_epic reducer refuses a parent cycle, a move of a
    -- repo-group sub-epic, and feed_append_only combined with group_by_repo or
    -- a feed role, each with the wording given here, against the rows the
    -- store holds and for every client that patches an epic (this tool,
    -- ReparentEpicFromTui, ToggleGroupByRepo). EpicService passes the patch
    -- through; it keeps only the checks that are about the request (an empty
    -- patch, the feed interval floor) and the not-found answer, including
    -- the existence of a named parent. So a call that names a missing parent
    -- answers not-found before the store's repo-group or cycle check runs.
    -- A store refusal reaches the MCP caller as a -32602 validation error
    -- carrying the store's own wording.
    requires:
        if parent_epic_id != omitted and parent_epic_id != null:
            exists core/Epic{id: parent_epic_id}
    -- The store's cycle walk climbs at most core/config.max_ancestor_walk
    -- hops above the proposed parent. A chain still going at that point is
    -- already a loop or a corrupt tree, and the move is refused with the same
    -- cycle error rather than waved through. ancestor_chain(p) (a black box:
    -- p, then its parent, and so on up to a root) counts p itself, so the
    -- bound admits p plus max_ancestor_walk - 1 ancestors above it.
    requires:
        if parent_epic_id != omitted and parent_epic_id != null:
            epic not in ancestor_chain(core/Epic{id: parent_epic_id})
            and ancestor_chain(core/Epic{id: parent_epic_id}).count <= core/config.max_ancestor_walk

    let prior_status = epic.status

    -- An auto-created repo-group sub-epic cannot be moved out of its
    -- grouping root. This covers BOTH reparenting (a new parent) and
    -- detaching (parent_epic_id = null): any non-omitted parent_epic_id on
    -- such an epic is rejected with a validation error, "Cannot reparent an
    -- auto-created repo-group sub-epic".
    requires:
        if parent_epic_id != omitted:
            epic.origin != repo_group

    ensures:
        if title != null: epic.title = title
    ensures:
        if description != null: epic.description = description
    ensures:
        if status != null: epic.status = status
    ensures:
        if plan_path != null: epic.plan_path = plan_path
    ensures:
        if sort_order != null: epic.sort_order = sort_order
    ensures:
        -- Entering done via the status param stamps the completion time,
        -- matching MoveEpicForward. It does not compete with an explicit
        -- sort_order in the same call: the two write different fields, so
        -- a caller can set both and get both. Leaving done writes nothing.
        if status != null:
            if status = done and prior_status != done:
                epic.completed_at = now
    ensures:
        -- feed_command supports three states: omitted (no change), a string
        -- (set), or explicit null (clear to NULL)
        if feed_command != omitted: epic.feed_command = feed_command
    ensures:
        -- feed_interval_secs supports three states: omitted (no change), an
        -- integer (set), or explicit null (clear to NULL)
        if feed_interval_secs != omitted: epic.feed_interval_secs = feed_interval_secs
    ensures:
        -- group_by_repo is a plain boolean set (absent = no change). Note
        -- that setting it here flips the flag ONLY — no task migration is
        -- performed. See the guidance below.
        if group_by_repo != null: epic.group_by_repo = group_by_repo
    -- An epic may be append-only OR grouped/role-routed, never both, and the
    -- restriction is permanent. Repo is a MIRRORING feed's key — its items are
    -- repo-scoped things carrying their own repo's URL. An append-only feed's
    -- items are events keyed by where in the code they fired, and the one url
    -- they carry is a configured repo root that exists only to resolve a local
    -- clone, so grouping would partition every item into a single sub-epic.
    -- Grouping by a constant. The supported way to cover several repos is one
    -- flat append-only epic per repo under a common parent. Evaluated against
    -- the POST-update values, so it catches the pair whichever flag arrives
    -- second and whether they arrive together or in separate calls; feed_role
    -- is not settable here, so it is read from the stored epic.
    -- See feeds.allium: AppendOnlyFeed for the full argument.
    --
    -- CHECKED ONLY WHEN THE CALL TOUCHES ONE OF THE FLAGS. The store's
    -- patch_epic evaluates the pair only when the patch sets feed_append_only,
    -- group_by_repo or feed_role, so an unrelated update (a title, a status)
    -- to an epic that already holds the combination — left by a restore or a
    -- board that predates the check — is not refused. Any write that would
    -- create or keep the pair through one of the flags is.
    requires:
        if feed_append_only != null or group_by_repo != null:
            let appends_only = feed_append_only ?? epic.feed_append_only
            let grouped = group_by_repo ?? epic.group_by_repo
            appends_only implies (not grouped and epic.feed_role = none)

    ensures:
        -- feed_append_only is a plain boolean set (absent = no change).
        -- Unlike group_by_repo this needs no migration counterpart: the flag
        -- changes only what FUTURE cycles do, and neither direction rewrites
        -- an existing task. See feeds.allium: AppendOnlyFeed for what each
        -- direction costs — in particular that turning it OFF hands the next
        -- cycle licence to remove every accumulated task the emission does
        -- not name.
        if feed_append_only != null: epic.feed_append_only = feed_append_only
    ensures:
        -- parent_epic_id uses triple-state semantics: omitted = no change,
        -- integer = reparent under that epic, null = make this a root epic.
        if parent_epic_id != omitted:
            epic.parent = if parent_epic_id = null: null else: core/Epic{id: parent_epic_id}
    ensures:
        -- The store's visibility change and workspace move, with their own
        -- authority checks, in the same update_epic transaction
        -- (UpdateEpicIsOneTransaction).
        if visibility != null: workspaces/SetEpicVisibility(epic, visibility)
    ensures:
        if workspace_id != null:
            workspaces/MoveEpicToWorkspace(epic, core/Workspace{id: workspace_id})

    @guidance
        -- updated_at is refreshed automatically by the database layer
        -- on any patch_epic call, so the rule does not assert it
        -- explicitly.
        -- The auto_dispatch flag is not exposed via the MCP update
        -- tool; it is toggled only from the TUI through ToggleAutoDispatch.
        -- feed_command and feed_interval_secs use triple-state JSON
        -- semantics: absent field = no change; explicit null = clear;
        -- string/integer value = set.
        -- parent_epic_id uses the same triple-state JSON semantics.
        -- Cycle detection walks the ancestor chain of the proposed parent;
        -- if the current epic appears anywhere in that chain the update
        -- is rejected with a -32602 validation error.
        --
        -- group_by_repo is a FLAG FLIP ONLY over MCP — intentional, but
        -- consequential. The regroup/flatten task migration (RegroupEpic /
        -- FlattenEpic) fires only from the TUI toggle path
        -- (ToggleGroupByRepo, the `R` key), which deliberately inlines
        -- update-then-migrate rather than reusing this patch path. An agent
        -- that turns group_by_repo ON here leaves every existing task sitting
        -- directly on the epic, unrouted into per-repo sub-epics; turning it
        -- OFF here leaves tasks stranded inside the repo-group sub-epics
        -- rather than pulled back to the parent. The epic reports the new
        -- flag and the board renders accordingly, so the mismatch is not
        -- obviously visible. Reconciliation requires either a manual TUI
        -- toggle (which runs the migration) or, for feed epics, the next feed
        -- sync (GroupedFeedUpsert / FlatFeedReconcile in feeds.allium, which
        -- self-heal in both directions).
        --
        -- An epic with origin = repo_group cannot have its parent changed by
        -- this tool at all — neither reparented nor detached to root (see the
        -- requires above). This keeps auto-created per-repo sub-epics inside
        -- the grouping root that owns them. Nor can its origin be changed,
        -- or a hand-made epic's be set to repo_group, by any patch, and a
        -- repo_group epic is never private (workspaces.allium:
        -- RepoGroupsStayPublic). A re-parent that changes who the epic is
        -- private to needs its creator, an admin or a super-admin
        -- (workspaces.allium: ReParentingKeepsTheHolder).
        --
        -- epic.completed_at = now is the same completion stamp used by
        -- tasks.allium's ConfirmDone and this file's MoveEpicForward. Staying
        -- in done, staying outside done, or omitting status entirely all leave
        -- epic.completed_at untouched. epic.sort_order is whatever the
        -- sort_order clause above set, in every case: no status transition
        -- reads or writes it.
}

rule ManagedFeedConfigViaMcp {
    when: McpManagedFeedConfig(reviews_command?, reviews_interval_secs?,
                               cve_command?, cve_interval_secs?)

    -- One tool both reads and writes the four managed-feed config settings
    -- (reviews/CVE feed commands and their poll intervals).
    --
    -- Each field is triple-state JSON: omitted = leave the stored value
    -- unchanged; explicit null = clear to null; a value = set.
    --
    -- A call with NO fields is a pure read: it changes nothing, does not
    -- re-provision and does not notify the runtime. A call with at least one
    -- field (null counts as a field) is a write. Either way the reply is the
    -- resulting config, with unset values reported as unset.
    --
    -- Both intervals are bound by the universal feed-cadence floor
    -- (core.allium: "Interval literals", CLAIM 2), rejected with a -32602
    -- validation error naming the field and the minimum. These settings are
    -- not a separate kind of cadence: they are COPIED onto the managed epics'
    -- feed_interval_secs by ProvisionManagedEpics, so a value the floor would
    -- refuse on an epic must not be reachable by writing it here instead.
    -- Nothing about a dispatch-provisioned feed makes a busy loop cheaper than
    -- a user-configured one.
    requires:
        if reviews_interval_secs != omitted and reviews_interval_secs != null:
            seconds(reviews_interval_secs) >= core/config.min_feed_interval
    requires:
        if cve_interval_secs != omitted and cve_interval_secs != null:
            seconds(cve_interval_secs) >= core/config.min_feed_interval

    -- One write per field the call names, all sent as one change: a command
    -- and its interval are never stored out of step (settings.allium:
    -- SetManagedFeedSettings). The stored rows are what this file's config
    -- block reads back.
    let writes = managed_feed_writes(reviews_command, reviews_interval_secs,
                                     cve_command, cve_interval_secs)
    ensures:
        if writes.count > 0:
            settings/SetManagedFeedSettings(writes: writes)

    -- After persisting a write, re-materialise the managed epic tree from the
    -- new config. A pure read skips this.
    ensures:
        if reviews_command != omitted or reviews_interval_secs != omitted
           or cve_command != omitted or cve_interval_secs != omitted:
            ProvisionManagedEpics()

    ensures: ManagedFeedConfigRetrieved()

    @guidance
        -- This is the only configuration path: persist the settings, then fire
        -- ProvisionManagedEpics and refresh the board. Feed commands are not
        -- newline-validated (they are shell scripts run via sh -c, matching
        -- feed_command on epics). The interval floor above stays here, at the
        -- request boundary; the provisioning itself is the module's
        -- ensure_managed_epics reducer (store-split.allium X4).
        --
        -- Annotations. The tool's MCP hints can say only one thing, and the
        -- no-argument call is a read while any other call is a write. The
        -- declared access class is idempotent: readOnlyHint = false (honest
        -- for the write path; a client must not auto-approve it as a read),
        -- destructiveHint = false (a later call can set any value back) and
        -- idempotentHint = true (repeating the same call leaves the same
        -- state). Declaring read_only would be wrong for the write path, which
        -- is the riskier error. The description says plainly that a call with
        -- no arguments only reads.
        --
        -- == Black boxes ==
        -- managed_feed_writes(...) is one write per field that is not
        -- omitted, keyed by that field's setting (reviews_feed_command,
        -- reviews_feed_interval_secs, cve_feed_command,
        -- cve_feed_interval_secs); an explicit null clears the key.
}

rule ReparentEpicFromTui {
    when: ReparentEpic(epic, new_parent?)

    -- parent validation: when a parent is chosen it must exist and
    -- introduce no cycle. A null new_parent makes the epic a root.
    requires:
        if new_parent != null: exists new_parent
    requires:
        if new_parent != null:
            epic not in ancestor_chain(new_parent)
            and new_parent != epic

    -- An auto-created repo-group sub-epic cannot be moved, in either
    -- direction: both a new parent and a null new_parent (detach to root)
    -- are rejected with the validation error "Cannot reparent an
    -- auto-created repo-group sub-epic".
    requires: epic.origin != repo_group

    ensures: epic.parent = new_parent
    ensures: epic.updated_at = now

    @guidance
        -- Triggered from the kanban board when the user presses `m` on an
        -- epic card (not when inside the epic view). A collapsible tree
        -- overlay lists only epics that are relevant reparent targets: it
        -- excludes the target epic and its descendants (cycle prevention at
        -- the UI layer), epics in Done status, and epics hidden by the repo
        -- filter (include/exclude) or by the lens's only-active filter.
        -- Epics on the board filter's hide-list stay listed, as in
        -- MoveTaskToEpic. The board search query is deliberately NOT
        -- applied here — this picker has its own input buffer, so inheriting
        -- the board query would hide otherwise-valid targets. See
        -- board_search_filter in board-layout.allium. When status filtering removes a
        -- parent but keeps
        -- an eligible child, the child is re-rooted to the top level so it
        -- stays selectable. The first item is always "— no parent —", which
        -- makes the epic a root.
        -- Tree navigation: j/k = up/down, l/→ = expand,
        -- h/← = collapse, Space/Enter = select. Space does not expand:
        -- it chooses, so it never reads as a fold key.
        -- The cursor opens on the epic's current parent (its ancestors are
        -- expanded so the row is visible); with none, or when that epic is
        -- not offered, it opens on "— no parent —".
        -- A [y/n] confirmation prompt is shown before committing the
        -- change. Pressing n returns to the picker; Esc/q cancels entirely.
        -- The confirmation is UI sugar — the normative outcome is the
        -- reparent itself, after the user has confirmed.
        --
        -- Cycle guards mirror UpdateEpicViaMcp: the chosen parent must not
        -- have the target epic anywhere in its ancestor chain, and the epic
        -- cannot be its own parent. The UI tree already excludes ineligible
        -- targets, so these guards are a defensive backstop.
        --
        -- The repo-group guard and the cycle guard are NOT UI-layer concerns:
        -- this flow patches the epic through the same path as
        -- UpdateEpicViaMcp, and the store's patch_epic refuses both (see that
        -- rule), so an attempt to move an origin = repo_group sub-epic fails
        -- there and the TUI reports the error. The picker does not pre-filter on origin, so
        -- a repo-group sub-epic can be selected and only then rejected.
        -- updated_at is refreshed automatically by the database layer on
        -- any patch_epic call; the assertion above documents the intent.
}

rule ToggleAutoDispatch {
    when: UserTogglesAutoDispatch(epic)

    ensures:
        epic.auto_dispatch = not epic.auto_dispatch

    @guidance
        -- Triggered by pressing U in the epic detail view (i.e. while
        -- ViewMode::Epic is active). The key is a no-op outside that
        -- view.
        -- Flips the auto_dispatch flag. When false, closing a subtask's
        -- agent session does not chain: AutoDispatchNextSubtask leaves
        -- the epic untouched and the next subtask is dispatched by the
        -- human from the TUI.
        -- The new value is persisted to the database and reflected
        -- immediately in the header indicator (green = auto, muted = manual).
}

-- == Automatic Subtask Chaining ==

rule AutoDispatchNextSubtask {
    when: pr/SessionClosed(task, close)

    -- Deliberately no requires. A chain that stops is a normal outcome, not a
    -- violated precondition, so every guard lives inside the ensures below:
    -- no epic, auto_dispatch = false, or no backlog subtask each leave the
    -- epic untouched rather than rejecting the trigger.
    let epic = task.epic

    ensures:
        if epic != null and epic.auto_dispatch:
            -- First backlog subtask, ordered by sort_order (nulls fall back to
            -- the task id), then by id, whose epic_chain request is accepted.
            -- Phoenix subtasks never are: the request refuses them
            -- (store-split.allium: RequestDispatch) — see
            -- PhoenixIsNeverChained below. Nor is a subtask still waiting
            -- for its scheduled time (schedules.allium, S8): the request
            -- refuses one whose next_due is ahead of now, in the same way,
            -- and the chain moves on to its next candidate.
            let next = first_by_order(epic.subtasks where status = backlog and not phoenix)
            -- A request, as every launch path writes
            -- (store-split.allium: RequestDispatch), naming the closed task's
            -- host — the host of the exit_session row, whose board serves the
            -- agent that closed. No mode: the module derives it from the
            -- subtask. A refused request moves the chain to the next
            -- candidate (see the guidance).
            if next != null:
                split/DispatchRequested(task: next, requester: close.host, origin: epic_chain)
                -- Recorded for exit_session's response, which names the
                -- subtask (mcp-task-tools.allium: ExitSessionViaMcp).
                close.chained_task = next

    @guidance
        -- Epic chaining IN THE MODULE
        -- (store-split.allium, W6). SessionClosed is emitted by the session
        -- close (ExitSession in pr-workflow.allium), which runs inside the
        -- request_exit_session reducer's transaction (store-split.allium:
        -- RequestExitSession), and this rule runs in that same transaction,
        -- after the terminal write. So the next subtask's worktree is always
        -- cut from a base_branch that already contains the finished subtask's
        -- work. Nothing of it runs on the board except
        -- ReportChainedDispatchFailure.
        -- close is the exit_session request row the close wrote
        -- (store-split.allium: SessionRequest).
        --
        -- exit_session is the ONLY emitter of SessionClosed, and that is the
        -- whole point. Reaching it requires a wrap_up first, and wrap_up is
        -- where the work is made visible to the next subtask: action = rebase
        -- rebases onto base_branch server-side, action = pr records the PR the
        -- work landed in, action = done is the caller stating the task needed
        -- neither. All three chain. What does NOT chain is any other route
        -- into Done — update_tasks(status = "done") (MarkTaskDoneViaMcp in
        -- mcp-task-tools.allium) and moving a card to Done in the TUI both
        -- close the task without emitting SessionClosed, because neither has a
        -- wrap_up behind it and so neither can say anything about base_branch.
        -- Chaining off those would launch a successor against an arbitrary
        -- base on the say-so of a caller who was never in the closing task's
        -- session.
        --
        -- SessionClosed does not follow every attempted close, only the ones
        -- that took effect. The close is one module transaction carrying the
        -- terminal status, the cleared tmux_window and this rule; when the
        -- call is refused or fails, none of it lands, SessionClosed is never
        -- emitted and this rule never runs. That is intentional, not a gap: the closing subtask is
        -- still sitting in its old status with its work unrecorded, and adding a
        -- freshly launched successor on top of that is strictly harder for a
        -- human to notice than the failed close on its own — the successor would
        -- also branch from a base whose fast-forward state the failed close says
        -- nothing about. The epic simply waits, exactly as it does for the
        -- normal stopping conditions below, and the human closes the subtask by
        -- hand and dispatches the next one from the TUI.
        --
        -- There is no agent-facing tool for this. Beside automatic chaining,
        -- an agent that dispatched the next subtask itself and then exited
        -- would fire TWO subtasks, one of them from a base that predates its
        -- own commits.
        --
        -- Ordering guarantee. Every write that determines the next
        -- subtask's starting base is complete before selection happens: the
        -- rebase / fast-forward of base_branch (WrapUpRebase) finished before
        -- its wrap_up row was done, which the close requires, and the closing
        -- task's terminal status is written earlier in the same transaction.
        -- The one thing still in flight is the closing task's tmux window
        -- teardown, which the host runs from the exit_session row after the
        -- transaction (pr-workflow.allium: ExitSessionTeardown) and which may
        -- overlap the new window's creation; window names are keyed by task
        -- id, so the closing and opening windows cannot collide. The
        -- guarantee is about store state, not OS-level teardown.
        --
        -- Five ways the chain stops, all normal outcomes: the task was closed
        -- by a route other than exit_session (see above); the closed task has
        -- no epic; the epic has auto_dispatch = false (see ToggleAutoDispatch);
        -- the epic has no backlog subtask left; or every backlog subtask it has
        -- left is a phoenix one (see PhoenixIsNeverChained). In each case the epic is
        -- untouched and the close reports a plain "session closed" response,
        -- and the human dispatches the next subtask from the TUI — the same
        -- recovery path auto_dispatch = false already produces. (A close whose
        -- terminal write failed also leaves the epic untouched, but that is a
        -- failure rather than a normal outcome and its response text says so;
        -- see above.)
        --
        -- PhoenixIsNeverChained. A subtask with phoenix = true is never a
        -- candidate, in any epic, whatever its position. Two reasons, and the
        -- first is a hard one:
        --
        --   1. It closes a loop that would otherwise have no floor. A phoenix
        --      subtask that finished would respawn into backlog (tasks.allium:
        --      PhoenixRespawn) and this rule would immediately dispatch the
        --      successor, which would finish, respawn, and be dispatched again
        --      — an epic that never runs out of work and never stops launching
        --      agents. The guard lives HERE rather than in PhoenixRespawn
        --      because the respawn is not the questionable half: creating a
        --      backlog task is always safe, and launching an agent at it
        --      unattended is the step that needs the veto.
        --   2. It is what a phoenix task means. The flag exists so a recurring
        --      task can be queued without picking its moment by itself — a
        --      human picks it, or the task's own schedule does
        --      (schedules.allium, S3). Finishing an unrelated sibling is
        --      neither, so the chain never launches one.
        --
        -- The skip is a filter, not a stop: the chain passes OVER a phoenix
        -- subtask and dispatches the next ordinary backlog subtask behind it.
        -- An epic whose only remaining backlog subtasks are phoenix ones stops,
        -- and that is the fourth normal stopping condition alongside the three
        -- below.
        --
        -- It belongs to the CHAIN and to nothing else. Human dispatch is
        -- deliberately unaffected: pressing Space on a phoenix backlog card
        -- claims and launches it exactly as it would any other, which is the
        -- entire point of the flag (see the head of the Phoenix section in
        -- tasks.allium — the recurrence is in the queueing, not the running).
        -- Concretely, the filter lives in the request reducer the chain and
        -- every other entry point share (store-split.allium: RequestDispatch),
        -- keyed on the request's origin: only an epic_chain request for a
        -- phoenix task is refused. The chain treats that refusal like any
        -- other and offers its next candidate; a request from any other
        -- origin takes a phoenix task as it would any other.
        --
        -- Candidate selection. Only direct Task children are candidates,
        -- ordered by sort_order ascending with the task id both as the fallback
        -- for nulls and as the tiebreaker. Sub-epics are NEVER auto-dispatched:
        -- if an epic's remaining work is nested inside a sub-epic, the human
        -- operator must dispatch that sub-epic explicitly from the TUI.
        -- Follow-up tasks created between wrap_up and the close (e.g. by the
        -- /retro skill) carry no sort_order, so their fallback key is their own
        -- — always larger — id: they sort after every planned subtask and are
        -- only ever chosen once the planned subtasks are exhausted.
        --
        -- At most one agent is launched per closed session, even under
        -- concurrent closes, and never two for one subtask. The chain claims through the same dispatch request every entry
        -- point writes (store-split.allium: RequestDispatch): it offers the
        -- candidates one at a time, in the order above, and the module
        -- accepts the first request whose task is still backlog with no open
        -- request. The offers are made inside the close's own
        -- transaction, so a refused candidate rolls back nothing but itself
        -- and the chain moves on. A refused request is ORDINARY — another entry point or a
        -- concurrent close got there first — and the chain moves to the next
        -- candidate; it is not a failure and is not reported. Two sessions
        -- closing on the same epic in the same instant therefore launch two
        -- DIFFERENT subtasks. Running out of candidates this way is stopping
        -- condition three (no backlog subtask left), not a further one.
        -- Exclusivity is the module's: DispatchClaimExclusive (dispatch.allium)
        -- binds this chain, DispatchTaskViaMcp and the TUI keys alike.
        -- The request names the closed task's host, which is the board the
        -- closing agent's exit_session call came through, so that board
        -- provisions the subtask (dispatch.allium: ProvisionDispatch). Its
        -- requested_by is the close's caller, who passed the same owner check
        -- (store-split.allium: RequestExitSession, D11). The request carries no
        -- mode, so the module derives it from the subtask: a plan-less
        -- research-tagged subtask gets the research agent.
        -- Consequence, on every path and not only this one: the claim applies
        -- backlog -> running with the default running sub_status and a seeded
        -- last_pre_tool_use_at ahead of worktree provisioning, so a task being
        -- dispatched is briefly running with worktree = null. Both crash
        -- detection and activity classification filter on tmux_window !=
        -- null, so that window produces no spurious Stale or Crashed (see
        -- agent-health.allium), and the wrap-up gate requires a worktree, so the
        -- subtask cannot be wrapped up before it is provisioned.
        --
        -- The chain learns the outcome FROM THE REQUEST ROW, not from a direct
        -- return. Its request carries origin epic_chain (store-split.allium:
        -- DispatchRequest.origin), and when that row ends failed,
        -- ReportChainedDispatchFailure below raises AutoDispatchFailed from the
        -- row itself — so nothing about the chain has to be remembered in
        -- memory, and a board restart while the subtask provisions (which
        -- fails the row "host restarted") still reports it. A refresh
        -- notification is sent to the TUI when the row finishes either way. A failed request has already had its claim
        -- released by the module (store-split.allium:
        -- ReleaseClaimOfFailedRequest), conditionally, so the subtask is back
        -- in backlog with the default backlog sub_status and the agent its
        -- claim started ended as launch_failed (agents.allium:
        -- EndAgentWhenLaunchFails), unless it was provisioned or moved on
        -- meanwhile. The chain releases nothing itself.
        --
        -- No chain failure can fail the close. This rule runs in the close's
        -- own transaction, after the closing task's terminal write, and the
        -- host tears its session down once the transaction commits, so
        -- failing here would roll back a close that should stand and tell the
        -- agent its session failed to close. So the failure travels
        -- to the OPERATOR instead of to the closing agent: a failed dispatch
        -- emits AutoDispatchFailed, which SurfaceAutoDispatchFailure turns into
        -- board-visible evidence. A missing epic stops the chain like the
        -- normal stops above. Inside the module the chain swallows every
        -- other problem too: it never fails the transaction, because failing
        -- it would roll the close back with it. Fail CLOSED: when the chain
        -- cannot tell whether to run, it launches nothing. Nothing requested
        -- this chain, so the conservative default is to launch no agent:
        -- failing open would start an agent on an epic whose operator may have
        -- turned chaining off. A store that cannot be reached fails the whole
        -- exit_session call, close included (ExitSessionViaMcp).
}

rule ReportChainedDispatchFailure {
    when: request: split/DispatchRequest.state transitions_to failed

    requires: request.origin = epic_chain
    -- Raised ONCE, by the requesting host's board: the row names that host,
    -- and its subscription is the one that holds it (sync.allium:
    -- SubscribeToThisHostsDispatchRequests). There is one board per host
    -- (store-split.allium: HostRestartFailsInterrupted), so no other board
    -- raises it too.
    requires: request.host = core/local_host()
    -- A subtask deleted under its request has no card to mark; deleting it
    -- was the operator's own act. (The delete also removes the row,
    -- store-split.allium: DeletingATaskDeletesItsRequestRows, so this board
    -- sees no "task deleted" failure to raise at all.)
    requires: exists request.task

    ensures: AutoDispatchFailed(
        task: request.task,
        epic: request.task.epic,
        reason: request.reason
    )

    @guidance
        -- Every failure reason reaches this rule the same way: a provisioning
        -- failure, the deadline stop ("deadline passed"), the module's
        -- backstop ("timed out"), or a host restart ("host restarted"). A
        -- restart is the case that needed the row: the restarting board fails
        -- the row itself on startup and sees that transition through its own
        -- subscription, so the chain's failure is reported although the board
        -- that started the chain is gone. A dispatched request ends the
        -- chain's work for this close and reports nothing.
        --
        -- The transition must be one this board sees. A row that failed while
        -- no board ran on this host (the backstop firing during a long
        -- outage) is already failed in the starting board's first look at
        -- the rows, which is history, and raises nothing (dispatch.allium:
        -- DispatchingFeedback, FailedRequestReported). By decision
        -- (store-split.allium, D12): the failed card shows it, and a chain
        -- stalled that way gets no ChainStallMarked.
}

rule SurfaceAutoDispatchFailure {
    when: AutoDispatchFailed(task, epic, reason)

    ensures: ChainStallMarked(task: task, epic: epic, reason: reason)
    ensures:
        if notifications_enabled:
            DesktopNotification(
                task: task,
                urgency: urgent,
                about: auto_dispatch_failed
            )

    @guidance
        -- AutoDispatchFailed is emitted by ReportChainedDispatchFailure when
        -- the request AutoDispatchNextSubtask wrote ends failed — the
        -- provisioning itself failed, its deadline passed, or its host
        -- restarted. The module has already released the claim by the time
        -- this fires, so the subtask is
        -- back in backlog and the epic has stopped progressing. Without this
        -- rule the only evidence is a warn line in the application log, which
        -- no operator is watching: an epic that stalls on a transient network
        -- failure looks identical to one that has run out of backlog subtasks.
        --
        -- Three surfaces, deliberately, because they fail at different
        -- distances from the board. The status message (see
        -- AutoDispatchFailureIndicator) reaches an operator who is looking at
        -- it; the desktop notification reaches one who is not, and is gated on
        -- notifications_enabled exactly as NotifyNeedsInput and NotifyReview
        -- are (agent-health.allium); ChainStallMarked is what remains once both
        -- have passed, and is the only one that is still there an hour later.
        -- Urgency is urgent rather than normal: unlike a review becoming
        -- available, nothing further happens on this epic until a human acts.
        --
        -- Nothing here reports back to the closing agent. Its session is
        -- already closed and its close already succeeded; see the "No chain
        -- failure can fail the close" paragraph in AutoDispatchNextSubtask.
        --
        -- A chain that stops before a subtask is selected (an unresolvable
        -- epic, or a store that cannot be reached, which fails the whole
        -- exit_session call) does not reach this rule, and
        -- that is a scope decision rather than an omission: neither can supply
        -- the task this rule marks.
}

rule ToggleGroupByRepo {
    when: UserTogglesGroupByRepo(epic)

    ensures:
        epic.group_by_repo = not epic.group_by_repo
    ensures:
        if epic.feed_command = null:
            -- group_by_repo is already flipped at this point, so
            -- true ⇒ just turned ON ⇒ Regroup; false ⇒ just turned OFF ⇒ Flatten.
            if epic.group_by_repo:
                RegroupEpic(epic)
            else:
                FlattenEpic(epic)

    @guidance
        -- Triggered by pressing R in the epic detail view (ViewMode::Epic).
        -- A no-op outside that view.
        -- The flag flip is unconditional: the new value is persisted
        -- immediately for any epic (feed or non-feed) and reflected in the
        -- header indicator (green "group:on [R]" when true, muted
        -- "group:off [R]" when false).
        --
        -- EXCEPT when the write is REFUSED. The keypress flips the board's own
        -- copy of the flag first and asks the write path second, so the header
        -- redraws before the outcome is known. The store's patch_epic refuses
        -- grouping on an append-only epic (UpdateEpicViaMcp's requires, which
        -- the toggle's write shares), and that is a refusal a
        -- user can reach by simply pressing R — the key is offered on every
        -- epic. On refusal the header is restored from the persisted epic, not
        -- merely accompanied by an error popup: otherwise the row would go on
        -- claiming "append-only  group:on [R]", a pair the system does not
        -- allow, until the next timed refresh happened to correct it. See
        -- AppendOnlyEpicIndicator.
        -- The flag write and the migration are ONE store reducer
        -- (SetEpicGroupByRepoIsOneTransaction), so a
        -- refused migration leaves the flag as it was; the board does not
        -- re-read the epic between the two.
        -- The task migration (RegroupEpic / FlattenEpic) only runs for
        -- non-feed epics (feed_command = null):
        --   * turning ON  → RegroupEpic moves every task into
        --     its per-repo sub-epic (creating sub-epics as needed);
        --   * turning OFF → FlattenEpic pulls every task from repo-group
        --     sub-epics back to the parent.
        -- For feed epics the flag still flips but no migration fires
        -- SYNCHRONOUSLY from the keypress: repo-grouping is driven by the feed
        -- ingestion itself, so triggering a migration directly from the toggle
        -- would duplicate that code path. The non-feed guard lives in the
        -- set_epic_group_by_repo reducer, and nowhere else: it reads the epic
        -- after the flag write and only runs RegroupEpic/FlattenEpic when
        -- feed_command = null. The two migration rules do not re-check it
        -- themselves (see their own guidance), so a feed epic is spared a
        -- migration because this caller declines to ask for one, not because
        -- the migration would refuse.
        -- Instead, BOTH directions are reconciled on the NEXT feed sync (auto
        -- poll or manual "r"), not synchronously on toggle:
        --   * turning ON  → GroupedFeedUpsert migrates pre-existing flat tasks
        --     into per-repo sub-epics (feeds.allium: "doubles as migration");
        --   * turning OFF → FlatFeedReconcile re-homes tasks from the epic's
        --     RepoGroup sub-epics back to the parent and deletes the now-empty
        --     sub-epics (feeds.allium), the OFF-side mirror of that migration.
        -- The toggle handler itself stays a pure flag flip for feed epics; the
        -- self-healing is poll-driven and symmetric in both directions.
}

rule SetEpicGroupByRepoIsOneTransaction {
    when: EpicGroupByRepoSetRequested(epic, group_by_repo)

    -- The board's group-by-repo toggle (ToggleGroupByRepo) is ONE
    -- reducer, set_epic_group_by_repo: it writes the flag and, for a non-feed
    -- epic, runs RegroupEpic (flag on) or FlattenEpic (flag off) in the same
    -- transaction, so a failure never leaves the flag set and the tasks
    -- unmoved.
    requires: exists epic

    ensures: EpicPatchAccepted(epic: epic, patch: { group_by_repo: group_by_repo })
    ensures:
        if epic.feed_command = null:
            if group_by_repo:
                RegroupEpic(epic)
            else:
                FlattenEpic(epic)

    @guidance
        -- The flag write is patch_epic's, so the append-only refusal is
        -- unchanged, and a refusal of any part leaves the epic and its tasks as
        -- they were. A missing epic is an error, as in update_epic.
        -- regroup_epic and flatten_epic stay as reducers: ingest paths and the
        -- MCP surface call them directly.
}

rule UpdateEpicIsOneTransaction {
    when: EpicUpdateRequested(epic, patch, workspace?, visibility?)

    -- The board's update of an epic (field patch, workspace move and
    -- visibility change) is ONE reducer, update_epic, so a refused part leaves
    -- nothing applied. A member who is not the creator sends a title and a
    -- visibility: the visibility is refused and the title is not saved, and the
    -- caller learns the whole update failed.
    requires: exists epic

    ensures: EpicPatchAccepted(epic: epic, patch: patch)
    -- A patch that names a feed_command is held to feeds.allium's
    -- FeedCommandAuthority (spacetime/module/src/tasks_epics.rs::may_change_feed_command).
    ensures:
        if patch.feed_command != null:
            EpicFeedCommandSet(epic: epic, command: patch.feed_command)
    ensures:
        if workspace != null: epic.workspace = workspace
    ensures:
        if visibility != null: epic.visibility = visibility

    @guidance
        -- workspace and visibility are absent to leave that part alone. The
        -- parts are patch_epic's, move_epic_to_workspace's and
        -- set_epic_visibility's, run in that order inside one transaction;
        -- their refusals are unchanged. A missing epic is an error here ("epic
        -- <id> does not exist"), unlike patch_epic's silent no-op, and the
        -- service maps it to NotFound without pre-reading the board's cache.
}

-- Repo grouping runs in the store (store-split.allium S1).
-- route_target is the store's. create_task and respawn_phoenix_successor
-- place a new task, and set_task_epic places a moved one, into the per-repo
-- sub-epic when the epic named is a grouped non-feed root (creating the
-- sub-epic if needed); any other epic is used as named. patch_task, when the
-- patch carries a repo_path, re-routes a task already inside a grouped
-- non-feed subtree to the sub-epic for its new repo and deletes the sub-epic
-- it left if that is now empty (re-scoping its learnings first,
-- learnings.allium: ReScopeLearningsOnRepoGroupDelete). Each does this inside
-- the same transaction as the write, and recalculates the epics it touched
-- there, so no client re-derives it and none can disagree. RegroupEpic and
-- FlattenEpic are the reducers regroup_epic and flatten_epic; the TUI toggle
-- only calls them.

rule RegroupEpic {
    when: RegroupEpic(epic)

    requires: epic.group_by_repo
    requires: epic.feed_command = null

    ensures:
        for task in epic.subtasks:
            task.epic = route_target(epic, task.repo_path)
            RecalculateEpicStatus(task.epic)
    ensures: RecalculateEpicStatus(epic)

    @guidance
        -- Each destination sub-epic is recalculated as tasks land in it (not
        -- only the grouped root): a sub-epic that just received tasks must
        -- leave done if it had settled there empty.
        --
        -- Both preconditions above are checked by the TUI caller
        -- (ToggleGroupByRepo, which flips the flag and then picks
        -- RegroupEpic vs FlattenEpic by its new value, and only for a
        -- non-feed epic). The regroup_epic reducer does not refuse an epic
        -- that fails them, but it cannot misplace a task on one either: its
        -- route_target answers the epic itself for any epic that is not a
        -- grouped non-feed root, so no task moves and only the epic's status
        -- is recalculated. There is no second caller today.
        --
        -- route_target(epic, repo_path) returns the existing per-repo
        -- sub-epic for that repo path, creating one (with origin =
        -- repo_group, always public: workspaces.allium:
        -- RepoGroupsStayPublic) if none exists yet. The sub-epic title is
        -- derived from the repo path's base name.
        --
        -- Every task this moves leaves its epic, so its unseen marks end
        -- (tasks.allium: UnseenMarkEndsWithTheTaskLeavingItsEpic;
        -- the store's relink_task clears them with the move). The same holds
        -- for FlattenEpic and for the repo_path-change reroute.
        -- A grouped epic should have no direct tasks after this runs:
        -- every task is routed into a sub-epic.
        -- RegroupEpic is idempotent: calling it when tasks are already
        -- in the correct sub-epic is a no-op for each task.
        -- Dispatching a grouped epic operates per sub-epic, not on the
        -- parent directly: only direct Task children are dispatch
        -- candidates (see AutoDispatchNextSubtask), so the human selects a
        -- sub-epic to dispatch. Sub-epics are one level deep — sub-epics
        -- of repo-group sub-epics are not created.
        --
        -- Progress aggregation agrees across surfaces: the TUI's
        -- epic-status indicator (●N counts by status column) aggregates
        -- across all children (tasks + sub-epics) recursively, and
        -- the MCP tools (GetEpicViaMcp, ListEpicsViaMcp) share one rollup
        -- that likewise aggregates every descendant sub-epic's tasks for a
        -- group_by_repo epic (direct subtasks only for any other epic). A
        -- grouped root whose tasks all live in per-repo sub-epics therefore
        -- reports the real totals to agents and to the board alike.
}

rule FlattenEpic {
    when: FlattenEpic(epic)

    requires: epic.group_by_repo = false
    requires: epic.feed_command = null

    ensures:
        for sub in epic.sub_epics where sub.origin = repo_group:
            for task in sub.subtasks: task.epic = epic
            -- (sub.subtasks.count evaluated after the task-move above, i.e. post-state)
            -- reduces to: sub had no nested sub-epics and all its tasks were just moved out.
            if sub.subtasks.count = 0 and sub.sub_epics.count = 0:
                DeleteEpic(sub)
            else:
                RecalculateEpicStatus(sub)
    ensures: RecalculateEpicStatus(epic)

    @guidance
        -- Every one of the sub-epic's own direct tasks is moved to the root
        -- before the emptiness check runs, so the only way it is left
        -- non-empty (DeleteEpic skipped) is nested sub-epics of its own —
        -- flatten_epic re-homes only direct tasks, one level. A sub-epic left
        -- non-empty this way IS still recalculated (the same shared cleanup
        -- helper recalculates whichever branch it takes: delete, or
        -- recalculate-and-keep), so its status reflects the tasks it just
        -- lost even though it survives.
        --
        -- As with RegroupEpic, both preconditions above are enforced by the
        -- TUI caller (ToggleGroupByRepo), not by the handler itself: the
        -- re-home walk runs unconditionally on whatever epic it is handed.
        --
        -- Moves all tasks from repo-group sub-epics back to the parent
        -- epic. Only sub-epics with origin = repo_group are affected;
        -- sub-epics the user created manually are left alone. A repo-group
        -- sub-epic that ends up with no tasks and no sub-epics is deleted.
        -- FlattenEpic is idempotent: calling it when no repo-group
        -- sub-epics exist is a no-op.
        -- Every task moved back to the parent ends its unseen marks
        -- (tasks.allium: UnseenMarkEndsWithTheTaskLeavingItsEpic),
        -- as in RegroupEpic.
        -- After flattening, RecalculateEpicStatus is called to update
        -- the parent's status based on its new direct children.
}

-- == Managed Feed Provisioning ==

rule ProvisionManagedEpics {
    when: ProvisionManagedEpics()

    -- Idempotently materialises the managed feed epic tree from the
    -- config surface above. Fires on TUI startup, reading the current
    -- reviews/CVE feed configuration, AND whenever the config is saved via
    -- ManagedFeedConfigViaMcp. Running it repeatedly converges on the same
    -- tree without creating duplicates: every managed epic is matched by the PAIR
    -- (feed_role, expected parent) — NEVER by title, so a user who renames
    -- any managed epic keeps that rename across re-runs. The parent half of
    -- that key means recognition is position-sensitive; see the reparenting
    -- caveat in the guidance below.
    --
    -- Two independent subtrees, each provisioned ONLY when its command
    -- is configured (opt-in):
    --
    --   reviews subtree (when reviews_feed_command != null):
    --     - a reviews_parent ROOT epic (parent = null) carrying the
    --       reviews feed_command + feed_interval_secs;
    --     - three role SUB-epics under it — my_reviews, team_reviews,
    --       bots — each with feed_command = null.
    --
    --   cve epic (when cve_feed_command != null):
    --     - a cve ROOT epic (parent = null) carrying the CVE
    --       feed_command + feed_interval_secs.
    --
    -- Per-role ensure policy: if an epic with that role already exists,
    -- keep it and leave its title untouched; for the command-carrying roles
    -- only, update feed_command/feed_interval_secs when the configured value
    -- differs. Otherwise create the epic and stamp its feed_role — including
    -- when the user deleted a previously-provisioned one.
    --
    -- A managed ROOT is the caller's own: epic_for_role of a
    -- root role finds only an epic CREATED BY the caller, and a root created
    -- here is private (visibility = private, created_by = the caller), so each
    -- board provisions a root of its own and never reuses or rewrites another
    -- person's. A found root the caller created that is still public is made
    -- private. The
    -- review sub-epics inherit privacy from their root.
    --
    -- epic_for_role is a black-box lookup
    -- SCOPED TO THE EXPECTED PARENT for that role (null for the two root
    -- roles, the reviews_parent root for the three review sub-roles): each
    -- returns the single Epic with the given feed_role AND that parent, in
    -- or null when none exists. Singularity is guaranteed
    -- by core/Epic.UniqueFeedRolePerParent (at most one epic per
    -- (parent, feed_role)). An epic carrying the role under some OTHER
    -- parent does not satisfy the lookup.

    ensures:
        -- Reviews subtree — opt-in on reviews_feed_command.
        if reviews_feed_command != null:
            -- reviews_parent ROOT (command-carrying).
            let existing_parent = epic_for_role(reviews_parent)
            if exists existing_parent:
                -- Keep title; reconcile only the command/interval.
                if existing_parent.feed_command != reviews_feed_command:
                    existing_parent.feed_command = reviews_feed_command
                if existing_parent.feed_interval_secs != reviews_feed_interval_secs:
                    existing_parent.feed_interval_secs = reviews_feed_interval_secs
            else:
                core/Epic.created(
                    title:              "PR Reviews",
                    status:             backlog,
                    parent:             null,
                    feed_role:          reviews_parent,
                    feed_command:       reviews_feed_command,
                    feed_interval_secs: reviews_feed_interval_secs,
                    created_at:         now,
                    updated_at:         now
                )

            -- Three role SUB-epics, all feed_command = null. They are
            -- reconciled only via the parent's role router; the FeedRunner
            -- never polls them independently. Their parent is the (now
            -- guaranteed to exist) reviews_parent root.
            let parent_root = epic_for_role(reviews_parent)
            if exists parent_root:
                for role in {my_reviews, team_reviews, bots}:
                    if not exists epic_for_role(role):
                        core/Epic.created(
                            title:        managed_role_title(role),
                            status:       backlog,
                            parent:       parent_root,
                            feed_role:    role,
                            feed_command: null,
                            created_at:   now,
                            updated_at:   now
                        )

        -- CVE epic — opt-in on cve_feed_command.
        if cve_feed_command != null:
            let existing_cve = epic_for_role(cve)
            if exists existing_cve:
                if existing_cve.feed_command != cve_feed_command:
                    existing_cve.feed_command = cve_feed_command
                if existing_cve.feed_interval_secs != cve_feed_interval_secs:
                    existing_cve.feed_interval_secs = cve_feed_interval_secs
            else:
                core/Epic.created(
                    title:              "CVE",
                    status:             backlog,
                    parent:             null,
                    feed_role:          cve,
                    feed_command:       cve_feed_command,
                    feed_interval_secs: cve_feed_interval_secs,
                    created_at:         now,
                    updated_at:         now
                )

    @guidance
        -- Where it runs. The whole provisioning run is
        -- ONE module reducer, ensure_managed_epics(reviews_command,
        -- reviews_interval_secs, cve_command, cve_interval_secs), so every
        -- lookup and create above lands in one transaction (store-split.allium,
        -- X4). The board still reads and writes the four settings, applies the
        -- feed-interval floor to them (request shape; ManagedFeedConfigViaMcp),
        -- and calls the reducer with the values it read.
        --
        -- No feed_command on sub-epics — the B3 concurrency guard.
        -- The my_reviews / team_reviews / bots sub-epics deliberately
        -- carry feed_command = null. Because FeedTick (feeds.allium)
        -- only polls epics where feed_command != null, the sub-epics
        -- are NEVER polled on their own. Their tasks are produced solely
        -- by the reviews_parent root's single emission, split across the
        -- buckets by the RouteSignals router (feeds.allium). Giving a
        -- sub-epic its own command would let two pollers write the same
        -- bucket concurrently — the race this guard exists to prevent.
        --
        -- Rename-stable, deleted-is-re-provisioned. Managed epics are
        -- matched by (feed_role, parent), never by title, so a user rename of any
        -- managed epic (root or sub) survives every re-run — only the
        -- command-carrying roles (reviews_parent, cve) ever have their
        -- feed_command/feed_interval_secs reconciled, and titles are
        -- never written for an existing epic. A managed epic the user
        -- deleted (epics.allium: DeleteEpic) is absent, so the next run
        -- creates it again for as long as its command is configured. The
        -- opt-out is clearing the command via managed_feed_config, not
        -- deleting the epic: there is no archived state for a
        -- deleted managed epic to linger in as a "do not resurrect" marker.
        -- A re-provisioned epic starts with no RetiredFeedItems — its
        -- predecessor's records went with it — so still-open items the user
        -- had deleted reappear (see DeleteEpic's reset guidance).
        --
        -- Opt-in. Each subtree is provisioned only when its command is
        -- configured. A null reviews_feed_command means no reviews_parent
        -- and no sub-epics; a null cve_feed_command means no cve epic.
        -- Clearing a command does NOT delete an already-provisioned
        -- subtree — teardown is a separate, explicit user action.
        --
        -- Idempotency & race-safety, and its limit. Re-running creates no
        -- duplicates AS LONG AS the managed epics still sit where
        -- provisioning expects them: each role is looked up as
        -- (feed_role, expected parent), and the store refuses a second
        -- epic with one role AT THAT SAME PARENT (core.allium:
        -- UniqueFeedRolePerParent; a root's role is unique per creator and
        -- workspace). Two boards starting up concurrently
        -- therefore cannot both insert the same managed epic: each board's
        -- run is one ensure_managed_epics
        -- transaction, the store runs the two one after the other, and the
        -- second finds every epic the first created and creates no second
        -- one (X4). The one
        -- exception is a database that already held
        -- duplicate managed epics before the index was fixed to be
        -- NULL-safe — that index was never built for it, so its
        -- provisioning stays unprotected until the duplicates are resolved
        -- manually.
        --
        -- Reparenting caveat (known consequence of the parent half of the
        -- key). Idempotency is NOT position-independent. If a user reparents
        -- a managed epic — moving a role sub-epic out from under the
        -- reviews_parent root, or making the reviews_parent root itself a
        -- child of something — the next provisioning run does not recognise
        -- it, because its parent no longer matches the expected one. The run
        -- then creates a FRESH epic for that role, which the unique index
        -- permits (the parent differs), leaving two epics carrying the same
        -- feed_role. The moved epic keeps its tasks; the new one starts
        -- empty. Recovering means reparenting the original back into place
        -- and deleting the duplicate by hand. Matching on feed_role alone
        -- would remove this hazard but would also make the (parent, role)
        -- index non-authoritative; the trade-off is unresolved (see the open
        -- question below).
        --
        -- managed_role_title(role) supplies the default display title for
        -- a freshly-created sub-epic (e.g. my_reviews -> "My Reviews",
        -- team_reviews -> "Team Reviews", bots -> "Bots"). It is consulted
        -- only on creation; once an epic exists its title is owned by the
        -- user.
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Epic Navigation
------------------------------------------------------------

-- Walking down one epic at a time is the slow way to reach work buried in a
-- deep tree. Three jumps shorten it, and one key undoes them. Every jump
-- opens an epic view whose "trail" is the chain of epic views between the
-- view the user pressed the key in and the epic landed on: one epic view per
-- level, each ancestor of the target below the starting view included.
-- Nothing the jump skips is lost — the trail is what makes q undo it a level
-- at a time, exactly as if the user had walked down by hand.
--
-- The epic tree below is read as it stands when the key is pressed. A jump
-- changes only which view is showing; no task or epic data changes.

-- The deepest epic whose own card the user would have had to open to reach
-- the work that earns `epic` its place in the column `status`. Starting at
-- `epic`: while the epic has no direct task with that status and exactly one
-- sub-epic whose subtree holds a task with that status, step into that
-- sub-epic. Stop when the epic has a direct task with that status, or when
-- two or more sub-epics hold such tasks (the branch point is the answer: the
-- user chooses there), or when no sub-epic does (an epic whose own status
-- came from its sub-epics' statuses with no task behind it stops here).
-- The repo filter and the other board-wide filters are not consulted: the
-- jump targets where the work is, not where it happens to be visible.
-- That definition is core/Epic.deepest_epic_with(status).

rule JumpToDeepestEpic {
    when: UserJumpsIntoEpic(epic, column_status)

    ensures:
        ViewShowing(epic.deepest_epic_with(column_status))
        -- The trail holds every epic from `epic` down to the target, so a
        -- target equal to `epic` is the ordinary single-level entry.

    @guidance
        -- Enter on an epic card, in any column and in any view (main board
        -- or inside an epic view). column_status is the column the card is
        -- in. Space on the same card keeps its meaning: it enters `epic`
        -- itself, one level, with no descent.
}

rule JumpToTaskEpic {
    when: UserJumpsToTaskEpic(task)
    requires: task.epic != null

    ensures:
        ViewShowing(task.epic)
        -- The trail runs from the epic directly below the view the key was
        -- pressed in (or from the root epic on the main board) down to
        -- task.epic. From a package view it starts at the outermost linked
        -- epic on task.epic's path, or at task.epic itself when none is
        -- linked (work-packages.allium: PackageViewCards).

    @guidance
        -- Enter on a task card in a FLATTENED column (board-layout.allium:
        -- BoardView.flattens). A task with no epic has nowhere to jump:
        -- the press does nothing. A flattened column can show a task from
        -- deeper than the current view, so the trail is longer than one
        -- level whenever task.epic is not a direct child of the current epic.
        -- In a column that is not flattened Enter keeps opening the task
        -- detail panel; `i` opens it in every column.
}

rule ExitEpicOneLevel {
    when: UserExitsEpicView(view)

    ensures:
        ViewShowing(view.parent)

    @guidance
        -- q (and Esc when no search is active) inside an epic view. Leaves
        -- exactly one level of the trail, whether the level was entered by a
        -- jump or by Space.
}

rule ExitAllEpics {
    when: UserExitsAllEpicViews(view)

    ensures:
        ViewShowing(MainBoard)

    @guidance
        -- Q inside an epic view returns to the main board whatever the depth
        -- of the trail. On the main board Q does nothing.
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Invariants
------------------------------------------------------------

-- No two feed tasks under one role-routed feed share an external_id. The feed
-- is a reviews_parent root, its three role sub-epics (my_reviews,
-- team_reviews, bots) and their repo-group children, so the identity holds
-- while role routing moves a task between role sub-epics and while repo
-- grouping is switched on or off. The store's feed reducers hold it: the feed
-- builds one existing-task index across the whole feed (the root, then the
-- repo-group children, then the role sub-epics, so a role sub-epic's copy
-- wins) and runs stale deletion over every role sub-epic and its children,
-- grouped or not.
--
-- routed_feed_root(t) is a black box: the reviews_parent epic whose role-routed
-- feed (as above) holds t, or null when no such feed holds it.
invariant FeedTaskIdentityPerRoutedFeed {
    for a in core/Tasks:
        for b in core/Tasks:
            a != b and a.external_id != null
                and routed_feed_root(a) != null
                and routed_feed_root(a) = routed_feed_root(b)
                implies a.external_id != b.external_id
}

-- EpicStatusRecalculation:
-- Epic status is persisted and recalculated by the module (recalculate_epic_chain).
    --
    -- WHERE IT IS DERIVED. Wherever the rows live, and by whatever holds all of
    -- them. On a single-machine install that is the local store and this has
    -- never been an interesting question. On a board sharing a store it is the
    -- STORE, and it has to be.
    --
    -- The reason is that no board sees all of an epic's children. An epic's
    -- subtasks can be owned by different people on different machines, and a
    -- board receives only what it subscribes to (sync.allium:
    -- SubscriptionsCoverOnlyTheOwnBoardAndItsEpics). A board deriving the
    -- status from what it can see is deriving it from a subset, so:
    --
    --   * a board that sees only the done children concludes "all children
    --     done" and writes done;
    --   * a board that sees a running one writes the regression back to
    --     backlog;
    --   * both are following this derivation correctly, and the epic flips
    --     between the two answers for as long as both boards are up.
    --
    -- That flapping is not a race to be tightened away with locking. It is two
    -- correct answers to two different questions, and the only fix is to stop
    -- asking the partial one. So the derivation runs once, at the store, over
    -- every child — and a board never computes it, not even for its own epics,
    -- because "its own" is not a property the epic has.
    --
    -- What a board still does is TRIGGER it: the trigger points below are
    -- mutations, the mutations go to the store, and the store recalculates as
    -- part of the same all-or-nothing change (sync.allium:
    -- EveryMutationIsAtomicAndAnswered). The new status therefore arrives at
    -- every subscribed board the same way any other row does, rather than being
    -- computed again on each of them. Nor does a board ask for a SEPARATE
    -- recalculation after a mutation: the board-side recalculation calls that
    -- repeated the module's own rollup (after hook events, after patch_epic
    -- and create_epic, after a close) do not exist
    -- (store-split.allium, X3), and the module is the only place that
    -- recalculates.
    -- It moves both forward and backward to always reflect child reality.
    -- "Children" means core/Epic.children: the union of subtasks (Task) and
    -- sub_epics (Epic). Each child contributes its .status value. Every child
    -- counts: no status takes a row out of
    -- the recalculation, so a finished child either sits in done or has been
    -- deleted. Epic progress totals likewise count every task.
    --
    -- Derivation logic:
    --   all children done (≥1 child) => done
    --   epic is done AND has non-done children => backlog  (regression)
    --   otherwise => no change (epic stays in current status)
    --
    --   The "no children" case does not auto-set to backlog — the epic
    --   keeps its current status. A freshly-created epic starts as backlog via
    --   the CreateEpic rule, so no information is lost.
    --   A child whose status this binary does not recognise stops the
    --   derivation: nothing is written, and the epic keeps what it has. Both
    --   guesses are wrong in a way nobody can see — treating it as done
    --   finishes an epic that is not finished, and treating it as unfinished
    --   holds one open forever with nothing saying why. Declining leaves the
    --   next recalculation, perhaps by a newer binary, free to get it right.
    --   The realistic producer is a colleague on a newer version, which only
    --   exists once the children can come from more than one machine.
    --   The running and review statuses are never set automatically; they are
    --   only reachable via manual MoveEpicForward / MoveEpicBackward actions.
    --   Manual placements (including running / review) are preserved across
    --   subsequent recalc calls — only the two auto-transitions above will
    --   override a manual placement.
    --
    -- The new status is written only when one of the two auto-transitions
    -- above fires; otherwise the DB row is left untouched.
    --
    -- The all-active-children-done => done auto-transition also stamps
    -- epic.completed_at = now, using the identical rule as tasks (see
    -- tasks.allium's ConfirmDone). The done-with-active-non-done-children =>
    -- backlog regression does NOT clear it: completed_at records the last
    -- completion, and a regression does not unmake one — the next
    -- all-children-done transition overwrites it. A no-op recalculation
    -- (status does not change) never touches it, mirroring the "DB row is left
    -- untouched" rule above. epic.sort_order is untouched by either
    -- transition: it is manual ordering and nothing else.
    --
    -- This is also the one write that can discard a manual Done-column reorder
    -- of the epic's card, which persists an override into the same field
    -- (tasks.allium, ReorderItem). Accepted: the epic genuinely finished at
    -- this moment, and a real completion outranks an earlier hand-placement.
    --
    -- Trigger points: the epic chain must be recalculated whenever the
    -- set of children or their statuses change. This includes:
    --   * task status transitions (patch_task, wrap_up, dispatch, etc.)
    --   * task creation or deletion within the epic
    --   * feed task upserts (ingest_feed) — both flat and grouped;
    --     for grouped feeds, each sub-epic's recalculation propagates upward
    --     to the parent automatically via the Propagation rule below.
    --   * sub-epic creation or status change
    --
    -- Propagation: after recalculating an epic's status, if the epic has a
    -- parent, the parent is recalculated as well.
    -- This propagates status changes up the full ancestry chain, to a bounded
    -- depth. The bound is not a modelling choice — no real ancestry approaches
    -- it — it is what turns a corrupt parent chain into one refused
    -- recalculation rather than an unbounded walk that takes the whole store
    -- down with it.
    -- The walk climbs at most core/config.max_epic_depth levels, so a parent
    -- cycle (which the store's reparent check already refuses) ends at that
    -- bound rather than looping.
    --
    -- Epic substatus (display label for epic cards):
    --   backlog + no plan             => unplanned
    --   backlog + has plan            => planned
    --   running + any child blocked   => "N blocked" (N = count of running
    --     tasks with sub_status in {needs_input, stale, crashed, conflict};
    --     running sub-epics do not directly contribute to blocked count)
    --   running + no blocked          => active
    --   review                        => in review
    --   done                          => done
    --
    -- Epic cards show colored status indicators (●N) for each non-zero
    -- child count by parent status, using the column palette colors.
    -- Sub-epic children are counted alongside task children in the indicators.

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Surfaces
------------------------------------------------------------

-- What the operator does to epics at the board. Each key's binding is in
-- keybindings.allium; the rule each action reaches says what it does.
surface EpicBoard {
    facing _: core/Operator

    provides:
        -- [E], the new-epic form (title, then description), finished. Inside an
        -- epic view the new epic is a sub-epic of the epic showing (CreateEpic).
        CreateEpic(title, description, parent?)
        -- [e] on an epic card (EditEpic).
        EditEpic(epic)
        -- [L] and [H] on an epic card (MoveEpicForward, MoveEpicBackward).
        MoveEpicStatus(epic, direction)
        -- [x] on one epic card (tasks.allium: DeleteKeyRouting), then y on
        -- its prompt (ConfirmDeleteEpic, DeleteEpic).
        ConfirmDeleteEpic(epic)
        DeleteEpic(epic) when epic.subtree_tasks.all(t => t.status = done)
        -- [m] on an epic card, the target confirmed (ReparentEpicFromTui).
        ReparentEpic(epic, new_parent?)
        -- [U] and [R] in an epic view (ToggleAutoDispatch, ToggleGroupByRepo).
        UserTogglesAutoDispatch(epic)
        UserTogglesGroupByRepo(epic)
        -- Enter on an epic card (JumpToDeepestEpic), or on a task card in a
        -- flattened column (JumpToTaskEpic).
        UserJumpsIntoEpic(epic, column_status)
        UserJumpsToTaskEpic(task) when task.epic != null
        -- q (or Esc with no search) and Q inside an epic view
        -- (ExitEpicOneLevel, ExitAllEpics).
        UserExitsEpicView(view)
        UserExitsAllEpicViews(view)
}

-- The module's epic-write reducers this spec guards, which any store client
-- may call (set_epic_group_by_repo, update_epic).
surface EpicWriteReducers {
    facing _: core/StoreClient

    provides:
        EpicGroupByRepoSetRequested(epic, group_by_repo)
        EpicUpdateRequested(epic, patch, workspace?, visibility?)
}

-- The epic tools of the dispatch MCP server. Replies name what they touch
-- (mcp-task-tools.allium: RepliesNameWhatTheyTouch).
surface EpicMcpTools {
    facing _: core/McpClient

    provides:
        -- create_epic (CreateEpicViaMcp).
        McpCreateEpic(title, description?, sort_order?, parent_epic_id?,
                      workspace_id?, visibility?)
        -- list_epics: one epic with epic_id (GetEpicViaMcp), else a list
        -- (ListEpicsViaMcp).
        McpListEpics(epic_id?, parent_epic_id?, recursive?, query?, limit?)
        -- update_epic (UpdateEpicViaMcp).
        McpUpdateEpic(epic, title?, description?, status?, plan_path?, sort_order?,
                      feed_command?, feed_interval_secs?, group_by_repo?,
                      feed_append_only?, parent_epic_id?, visibility?,
                      workspace_id?)
        -- managed_feed_config (ManagedFeedConfigViaMcp).
        McpManagedFeedConfig(reviews_command?, reviews_interval_secs?,
                             cve_command?, cve_interval_secs?)

    @guidance
        -- The caller is an agent or a session (core/McpClient). What a client
        -- may see or change is the store's to decide (workspaces.allium).
}

surface AutoDispatchFailureIndicator {
    facing user: core/Operator

    -- What ChainStallMarked (see SurfaceAutoDispatchFailure) looks like on the
    -- board: a subtask whose chained dispatch failed and was released back to
    -- backlog. Evaluated per rendered card, not only for the selected one.
    --
    -- The marked set is implementation-internal and is NOT persisted. It does
    -- not need to be: the board that raised AutoDispatchFailed holds it, and
    -- a board that has been restarted since is observing the epic afresh
    -- rather than replaying a stall it has already forgotten.

    context task: core/Task

    let chain_stalled = auto_dispatch_failed(task)

    exposes:
        chain_stalled

    @guarantee StatusMessageNamesTheSubtask
        -- The failure raises a status message naming the subtask, so an
        -- operator watching the board learns which chain stopped and why
        -- rather than only that something did. It is an ordinary transient
        -- message and expires on the usual status_message_expiry; the card
        -- indicator below is what carries the fact afterwards.

    @guarantee PersistsUntilRedispatched
        -- Unlike every other transient board signal, the card indicator does
        -- not decay on a timer. A stalled chain stays stalled until a human
        -- acts, so the evidence stays until a human acts: it is cleared when
        -- the subtask is dispatched again, when it leaves backlog by any other
        -- route (someone moved it, or another entry point claimed it), and
        -- when the task itself is removed from the board. Nothing else clears
        -- it. This is the whole point of the surface — a chain that died at
        -- 02:00 must still be visible at 09:00.

    @guarantee DispatchingOutranksIt
        -- While a dispatch is in flight for the subtask, the card shows the
        -- dispatching feedback (DispatchingFeedback in dispatch.allium), not
        -- this indicator. Retrying is the resolution, so a stale failure must
        -- never mask the retry's own spinner.

    @guarantee DistinctFromOrdinaryBacklog
        -- The indicator replaces the ordinary backlog age/plan indicator and
        -- is rendered in the same alarming style as the conflict, crashed and
        -- unprovisioned indicators. A released subtask is otherwise
        -- byte-identical to one that was never dispatched, which is precisely
        -- the confusion this removes.

    @guidance
        -- The epic header's auto-dispatch indicator (green = auto, muted =
        -- manual; see ToggleAutoDispatch) says only whether chaining is ON. It
        -- cannot say whether chaining is WORKING, and an operator reading a
        -- green indicator over a stalled epic is being actively misled. This
        -- surface is where "the chain tried and failed" lives.
}

surface AppendOnlyEpicIndicator {
    facing user: core/Operator

    -- What core/Epic.feed_append_only looks like on the epic header: the
    -- literal text `append-only`, in green, in the top indicator row.
    --
    -- That row already answers "what kind of epic is this" -- auto or manual
    -- dispatch (ToggleAutoDispatch), the feed routing role, repo grouping
    -- (ToggleGroupByRepo). Append-only is the remaining answer of that kind,
    -- and the one with the most visible consequence: such an epic's cards
    -- never clear, because absence from an emission removes nothing
    -- (feeds.allium: AppendOnlyFeed). Without a marker, a user looking at an
    -- epic whose cards only ever accumulate has nothing on screen explaining
    -- why.

    context epic: core/Epic

    -- Rendered only for an epic that has declared itself append-only.
    let visible = epic.feed_append_only

    exposes:
        visible

    @guarantee RenderedOnlyWhenTheFlagIsTrue
        -- The marker exists only for an epic whose flag is true; there is
        -- deliberately no `append:off` counterpart. This is the one place the
        -- row is asymmetric -- the grouping indicator always shows both
        -- states, "group:on [R]" and "group:off [R]" -- and the asymmetry is
        -- the point: nearly every epic is a mirroring one, so a false-state
        -- badge would be permanent clutter on the ordinary case in exchange
        -- for saying nothing about it.

    @guarantee NoKeyHintBecauseThereIsNoKey
        -- The marker carries no bracketed key, unlike "auto dispatch [U]" and
        -- "group:on [R]". In this row a bracket means "press this key to
        -- toggle", so its absence is what tells the user this is a property to
        -- read, not a control to press. That there is no keybinding for the
        -- flag is itself deliberate: turning it OFF gives the next feed cycle
        -- licence to remove every accumulated task the current emission does
        -- not name, which for a log feed is potentially all of them. The flag
        -- therefore belongs to wiring an epic up -- the MCP write path,
        -- UpdateEpicViaMcp -- rather than to a one-key board toggle. See
        -- feeds.allium: AppendOnlyFeed for the full argument.

    @guarantee SitsBetweenTheRoleAndGroupingSlots
        -- Position in the row: after the feed-role slot ("role:<role>", shown
        -- when feed_role != none) and before the grouping slot. The settled
        -- row is "manual dispatch [U]  append-only  group:off [R]", because
        -- UpdateEpicViaMcp refuses append-only together with grouping or a
        -- feed role and so neither neighbour's populated form is ever
        -- PERSISTED alongside the marker.
        --
        -- Never persisted is not the same as never rendered: pressing R on an
        -- append-only epic redraws the header before the refusal arrives. A
        -- marker whose job is to explain an epic must not be the thing that
        -- misleads about it, so that refusal restores the header rather than
        -- only reporting the error -- see ToggleGroupByRepo, which owns the
        -- mechanism and the argument for it.

    @guarantee ShownWhateverTheFeedCommand
        -- The flag is the only condition; there is no additional guard on
        -- feed_command. An epic carrying the flag with no feed command is a
        -- misconfiguration, and one the user is better off seeing than not --
        -- the marker is the only place that combination becomes visible.

    @guarantee EpicDetailViewOnly
        -- Like every other indicator in this row it renders in the epic detail
        -- view and never in board view (ViewMode::Board), which has no single
        -- epic to describe.
        --
        -- "Epic detail view" here means ViewMode::Epic, whatever the
        -- FLATTENED toggle is set to. Flattened is an orthogonal concept in
        -- this spec set — board-layout.allium's flattened mode applies inside epic view
        -- too, where it widens the scope to the epic's whole subtree — and it
        -- does not change which epic the header describes. So a flattened
        -- epic view still shows the marker, and only leaving for board view
        -- takes it away.

    @guarantee ReadOnlyWithNoTrigger
        -- Purely a readout: rendering it changes no state, and there is no
        -- trigger this surface responds to. The flag is written only by
        -- UpdateEpicViaMcp and read only by the feed cycle
        -- (feeds.allium: AppendOnlyFeed) and by this row.

    @guidance
        -- Rendered by src/tui/ui/shared.rs::render_top_indicators, the same
        -- function that draws the dispatch-mode, feed-role and grouping slots,
        -- so all four share one line and its width handling rather than
        -- competing for space from separate call sites.
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Open Questions
------------------------------------------------------------

open question "R is offered on every epic in the detail view, including an append-only one where UpdateEpicViaMcp guarantees the toggle will be refused. AppendOnlyEpicIndicator argues that a bracketed key in the header means 'press this to toggle', so advertising [R] there contradicts the row's own convention -- yet the flag it would refuse is exactly the one the append-only marker exists to explain. Should the keypress be guarded before it is attempted (no refusal, no rollback, but the service rule is then duplicated in the TUI), should [R] render differently on such an epic, or is refusing-then-restoring the honest behaviour to keep?"

open question "Managed-epic role lookup keys on (feed_role, parent), so reparenting a managed epic makes the next ProvisionManagedEpics run create a duplicate epic for that role. Should the lookup fall back to feed_role alone, should managed epics be reparent-blocked the way repo-group sub-epics are, or is the duplicate an acceptable cost of a user moving them?"
